    "qualityRules": [
      "Animation fundamentals are the baseline skills of making motion feel intentional, readable, and purposeful across 2D, 3D, and simple object animation.",
      "The principles of animation are proven motion laws—timing, anticipation, staging, follow-through, and more—used to make movement believable and appealing.",
      "Timing is the duration of an action across frames, controlling perceived mass, energy, and emotional temperature.",
      "Spacing is the distribution of in-betweens that shapes velocity curves—slow-ins, slow-outs, and linear vs. organic motion.",
      "Anticipation is a brief opposite or preparatory motion that prepares the eye for a primary action.",
      "Squash and stretch is temporary shape change that preserves volume to communicate elasticity and force.",
      "Follow-through is residual motion of appendages or secondary parts after the primary mass has stopped.",
      "Secondary action is additional motion that supports the primary action’s story and emotion while remaining subordinate.",
      "Staging is the clear presentation of an action or idea through pose, composition, contrast, and timing so intent is unmistakable.",
      "Appeal is the combination of design clarity, charming motion, and tasteful restraint that makes characters or graphics engaging.",
      "Character animation is performance-driven motion of a character that communicates intent, emotion, and personality through pose and timing.",
      "Product animation is purposeful motion of a product—turntables, feature callouts, assembly, or micro-interactions—that clarifies value."
    ],
